<p>To be fair:</p>
<p>The first simply isn't true. If a brightly-colored or reflective surface is right behind the half-silvered surface, it might work; otherwise, the light returning from the other side just isn't powerful enough to ovecome the light bouncing off the near side.</p>
<p>The second is something the author should consider, if only for the sounds (or lack of sounds) in the room beyond. (They haven't noted any mouth movement, after all.)</p>
<p>Image enhancement software has definite limits; as the available information is reduced, the interpolations get more and more approximate. Low-light cameras might work, but only under the same conditions as given in the article; they amplify all of the light.</p>

